\f1\fs22 Jamison Hardy\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Professor Hamish Hermann, the head lab-coat from his Bio-Weapons Division. \'93We have manufactured 
\f2\i the virus
\f1\i0 .\'94 \
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93It targets and kills all known human pathogens. The TPlusVirus is an actual panacea. \'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93A world without disease,\'94 his faced beaming with pride.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Professor Hamish Hermann was a hyperactive basket case when Jamison and his entourage arrived, but he looked great. His skin was glowing, rosy red cheeks, with the age lines, bumps and weird lumps that had appeared over the years, all gone. It was not only his skin, he stood tall, bolt-upright and muscular, like a marine in boot camp. This was not the same frail dweeb Jamison had lunch with a few weeks prior. He was wholly riddled with good health. \
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Yeah\'85.I gotta say I am very concerned. You or, rather we, will be destroying entire industries. Not to mention over population. Where are we going to put all these healthy fuckers?\'94 Jamison said appearing to be joking but was not. \
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Well, we won\'92t have to put them in hospital, ever.\'94 He wasn\'92t going to let his boss bum him out. He had achieved what no one else in human history had achieved. How many people could say that?\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Does everyone here know what\'92s going on?\'94 Jamison said, hoping that the Professor had implemented some security measures.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93My team and I have told no-one else of our conclusions, however, others must suspect something. Everyone must have noticed I am looking younger by the minute.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93How does it work?\'94\uc0\u8232 \
\pard\pardeftab720\fi360\sl220\qj\partightenfactor0
\cf2 \cb3 <$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93We don\'92t know exactly. It seems to optimize every biological process within every cell of the body. Thinking is clearer, memory perfect, breathing easier and taste \'96 in fact all senses have been fine tuned. The immune system is on hyper-drive, it destroys invaders efficiently, mercilessly, but causes no anaphylaxis. I don\'92t need glasses anymore. Both short and long sight are far superior. I see, hear and remember everything.\'94\cb1 \
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93How did you stumble across it? I can\'92t imagine you did it on purpose.\'94 
\f2\i Surely no one would be stupid enough to do this on purpose.
\f1\i0 \
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93It is a by-product of our bio-weapons research. As you are no doubt aware we succeeded in creating biological devices that target specific sections of the human genome and we were working on phase two, devices targeting specific individuals. We were trying to make the virus hardy enough to survive without water or air so we used \cb3 etremomorph\cb1  DNA to horizontally transfer genetic traits, but instead of making the virus impervious to attack, it causes the host to become impervious.\'94 The professor was the happiest camper Jamison had ever seen, but he clearly had no idea what he had done. \
\cb3 <$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Jamison was perfectly aware of the target-specific virus research. He ordered the research himself, but the concept had not been his idea. For a couple of centuries it had been the dream of depots of all kinds to secure control of a virus that would target his enemy. The old nation of China had various and numerous failures in trying to target the Caucasian race. One city in central China (find the location Wuhan) China was the hub of all bio-weapons research, but had the cover of being the largest manufacturer of medical masks, supplying 80% of the the entire world\'92s need. The city itself consumed 10% of all the world\'92s masks, a most peculiar statistic 
\f2\i (what were those fuckers up to?) 
\f1\i0 that only became relevant after numerous containment failures of their viral catastrophes. Although the release of the bird-flu and swine flu had been deliberate, just to test the waters, SARS and MERS somehow escaped containment and set about indiscriminately killing their countrymen. The final straw was the premature release of COVID-19, a virus that had promised to target Caucasians, wiped out half of the city and then on a rampage world wide. The death rate was nothing like they were aiming for, but it well truly fucked the economies of most countries, including their own. The epi-centre of the outbreak made it clear where the manufactured virus had originated and the standard denial were greeted as lies. A whistle-blower clinician had tried to get the word out, but even though he was a health man in his prime, he somehow succumbed to the disease, quickly and silently. The research was shut down and all involved were re-educated. \
\pard\pardeftab720\fi360\sl220\qj\partightenfactor0
\cf2 \cb1 <$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Jamison had no problem picking up the research. The pay-off for success was just too big to ignore.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Can you die?\'94 Jamison hoped so.\
\pard\pardeftab720\fi360\sl220\qj\partightenfactor0
\cf2 \cb3 <$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Not from disease or even catastrophic injuries although, no doubt, I will die of old age eventually. Wounds and broken bones repair themselves at 10 times, no 20 times the normal rate. I can hold my breath for an inordinate period of time. Hours and it doesn\'92t seem to affect me. I suspect the body simply draws oxygen out of the air directly through the skin \'96 the adaptive abilities of this organism are amazing, although, if I was blown up or torn into small pieces, I think my life would be irretrievable.\'94 \cb1 \
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93How many are infected?\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Just the research team, four all here.\'94 He fibbed. \'93It can only be transferred through the exchange of bodily fluids, blood and semen.\'94 \
\cb3 <$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93It\'92s an STD?\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Yep. The clap that will save the world.\'94\cb1  Professor Herrman was a now laughing loudly.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93I see the virus has had no effect on your sense of humour.\'94 Jamison kindly pointed out.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93What\'92s next, sir?\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93We will have to keep this under wraps. This facility goes into total lock-down. You and your team cannot leave, under any circumstances. We do not want this virus getting out.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93I understand. What will I tell them?\'94 His team would not be very happy. They had discussed fleeing the jurisdiction, but President Kruger\'92s war on everything had recently shut down all commercial air traffic, worldwide, until he could work out what was going on. Professor Hermann accurately predicted that Kruger would never work out what was going on and international flights would not be re-scheduled for quite some time. They would be trapped in Ulyssia and hunted down. No, their best chance was to stay put and for the time being, play ball.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Tell them not to worry, that everything is under control. Tell them we have received credible intelligence about a terrorist plot to destroy this facility and it has been locked down for their safety. Do not tell anyone else about the virus.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Of course, sir.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93One more thing. I would like to be infected, Professor.\'94 As soon as he got back to his building he planned to jump back into his Full Body Scanner and test the Professor\'92s claim. His results were pretty much the same from one day to the next and if the Professor was right, the results would be wildly different to this morning\'92s readout.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93I\'92m not that sort of man, sir. I don\'92t think I could manage, you know?\'94 The prof was red faced and genuinely embarrassed.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Professor, I was thinking more along the lines of an injection.\'94 The look on the professor\'92s face was priceless and Jamison could not help but be amused. \
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93The easiest way for me to cut my finger and jab you in your eye. Are you up for that?\'94 \
\cb3 <$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93No. An injection will be fine.\'94 He had already rolled up his sleeve and was waiting. \'93Hurry up, I haven\'92t got all day. It\'92s nearly 6.00am already.\'94\cb1 \
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>The professor moved quickly, smoothly, with purpose and although he did not have a vial of virus just lying around, in a bewildering blur of activity Jamison was inoculated and then out the door. \cb3 He waited a short time, before one of Jamison\'92s staff made and appearance. \'93Who are you?\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Call me Kleo,\'94 he said.\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\fi360\sl220\qj\partightenfactor0

